helbig@astro.multiCLOTHESvax.de (Phillip Helbig---remove CLOTHES to reply) writes:

>In my quest to collect interesting hardware, I sometimes end up with 
>stuff which is too old, big, slow, power-hungry etc for my needs.  Such 
>a beast has ALPHAserver 2000 4/200 on the front, but the display says 
>233 instead of 200.

Someone replaced the CPU without updating the label on the front (quite
common)

>It has two 9-pin serial ports (like the ALPHAstation 255/233) labelled 1 
>and 2.  It ALSO has a graphics card.

Connect a terminal to COM1 with a PC 9 pin to whatever cable, 9600 8/N/1.
Pound on the return key and you should see a prompt.

>I don't see a console prompt on a VT320 set to the standard settings
>(receive=transmit=9600, 8 bits no parity etc).  It seems to power up and 
>run through the self-tests OK, based on the LCD display.

Remove the keyboard or hit the return key while it's doing the selftest.

>I tried a |d|i|g|i|t|a|l| BCC08 which has "CONSOLE" written on the big 
>end and "PR1" written on the other end.  Should I expect this cable to 
>work?

No.

(at one time, Digital decided to use 9 pin connectors for certain MV2s.
They came up with a wiring scheme.  Some time later, PC makers decided
to do the same thing, and came up with their own wiring.  Guess which
one is now the 'standard' one?)

>Is it possible that the console is set to graphics?  If so, how can I 
>change it (preferably without having to pull the graphics card).

Once you see the >>> prompt on the terminal, enter the command
"set console serial".

>Which of the serial connectors should be the console (OPA0:)?

COM1.

-- 
-Mike


