This is a great question.

Generally, the school of thought on this says turn it on and leave it on.
The hardware warms up and then stays warm all the time.

Turning equipment on then off takes it through a thermal cycle, everything
expands and then contracts.and as the machine is made with different
materials, everything expands and contracts at different rates. It is said
that this cycle can lead to premature failure.

All my gear is left on, except when I go on holiday.

There are VAX and Alpha systems thta have been running fault free for years,
never turned off. There are VAX and Alpha systems that have been turned on
and off and have never had a fault. Your choice.

rtt




