Oh, one of my old buddies, the 2100 that is.

There were a few different models, two Pedastals and a couple of Rack Mount.

1. All Alpha systems, be they EISA or PCI need to have the correct ECU run
on them when changing from OpenVMS/OSF/1/Tru64 UNIX to Windows NT or vice
versa. The hardware controllers in the machines are EISA based programming,
so serial ports, parallel ports, console operation, onbaord video operation,
IRQ's, the ECU set's this up properly.
I remember a situation where a customer could not get any of the floppy disk
drives to show up on his AS4100's. The units had been built originally with
Windows NT, then later reloaded with Tru64 UNIX. A complete mystery to all
it seemed. I asked, have you run the correct ECU, and was told by all those
in the know, NON-DIGITAL service staff with shit for brains, the ECU does
nothing it never needs to be run. After a very frustrating argument I
convinced them to run the ECU on one machine. Oh looky, looky, a floppy disk
drive in Tru64 UNIX. Once all machines had been done, I think around 20 or
so, they were happy. I never got an apology, but I sure wnated to charge
them $1200.00 per machine fo rthe fix.

RUN THE ECU, I posted both at www.driverguide.com and this site is now
mentioned in about 10 different news entries here, a search at google would
do you.

2. The AS2100 first came out with 6 EISA slots and I seem to recall 3 PCI
slots. The second model was 6 PCI and 3 EISA. This from a bad memory, so
don't quote me verbatim on this.

3. The original deal is this, an Alpha only runs Alpha binaries. PC cards
contain X86 binaries. WHen the POST is run on a PC it runs the BIOS to
initilize the hardware and then finds the extended binaries on option cards
and runs that to initilialize that hardware such as SCSI and Video. SO what
we did was we bought Compaq Q-Vision cards because the X86 code was very
well documented and we re-wrote the code in Alpha binaries and put the code
to execute in the Alpha's Frimware. The X86 code never got executed, it got
turned off, another feature of the card and the Alpha binaries ran and
initialized the card. You need to use a specific COmpaq Q-Vision, and I
can't remeber the number. Now some folks will start to say, but, but, but,
and today you can say but. You see, we knew that this type of thing would
get us to market, notice the extreme lack of supported hardware on our early
models folks, all the init code is in the firmware for all that PC hardware,
aka, special EISA Adaptec code and the like, plus all kinds of noise
problems too. The AS2100 you will note has wave guides built in to it thta
pass the wiring around in the cabinet. The wave guides trap the EMI and RFI.
It also has a presureized plenum too, that makes for good airflow over the
modules for cooling and it has a airplane wing instead of a heatsink to cool
some bus chips on the memory controller. Oh yeah back to the firmware. All
the while there were smart boffins in the back rooms working on hte init
code problem and what they came up with was an X86 emulator that ran out of
firmware. SO you see, later machines could run the X86 code to init cards
cause what was really running was an emulator.

Whats my point you ask?

Until you make sure you have the correct supported hardware only in the box,
and until you run the corret ECU, there is no point in proceeding.

Anyone want my two cents on the Virtual Machine that was done to run X86
code under Windows NT and the pain that MS caused, let me know.

rtt

I demoed the first 2100 in North America, fresh off the production line in
Kanata to the people at Corel and they bought lots of them.




