I'm wondering what a good rule of thumb would be, both for small 
terminals like the VT320 but also for large colour graphics monitors.  I 
want to maximise the lifetime.  Obviously, leaving them switched on 
constantly is not ideal, but neither is switching them off as soon as 
one takes a short break and switching them back on again.  There must be 
some crucial time: if the device will be out of use for at least a 
certain period of time, then it should be switched off.

In all cases, a screen saver kicks in relatively quickly, so even if 
they stay switched on, the screen will be dark or displaying a rapidly 
changing pattern (so burn-through is probably not an issue).

Less urgent, but what would be the corresponding time for a workstation?
For a hard disk?  (Most of my hobbyist machines are running all the time
(if they run regularly at all).  Some I just use as a glorified
X-terminal, mainly to log in somewhere else.  In such cases, I could
switch off just the monitor, the workstation as well or even the BA350
and its disks.



