On 2004-03-28, Phillip Helbig---remove CLOTHES to reply <helbig@astro.multiCLOTHESvax.de> wrote:
> In my quest to collect interesting hardware, I sometimes end up with 
> stuff which is too old, big, slow, power-hungry etc for my needs.  Such 
> a beast has ALPHAserver 2000 4/200 on the front, but the display says 
> 233 instead of 200.

It's a 233. The CPU has been upgraded, and the sticker on the front wasn't
replaced. The display is set by firmware when it's figured out what kind of
machine it is.

> I plan to find a good home for this, since it is too big for me and, if 
> I read the labels correctly, consumes too much power.  (If I were rich, 
> I would keep it just for the sake of nostalgia.)

The power consumption on the back is a maximum value; unless the machine is
stuffed full of memory and CPUs and disk drives and expansion cards, it
won't draw anywhere near that much.

> I'm trying to get to the console prompt, then want to boot VMS just to 
> make sure everything is OK, see how much memory it has etc.

See my previous post. Boot it with the HALT switch in. Once you've got the
SRM prompt (>>>), then do "show config", and you'll get all the details.

> I tried a |d|i|g|i|t|a|l| BCC08 which has "CONSOLE" written on the big 
> end and "PR1" written on the other end.  Should I expect this cable to 
> work?

no. That cable is for a VAXstation 2000 only, and has funny wiring. A
standard 9-pin serial connection will work, however; it has the same pinout
as a PC.

> Is it possible that the console is set to graphics?  If so, how can I 
> change it (preferably without having to pull the graphics card).

If you boot the machine without a keyboard plugged into the PS/2 keyboard
port, it'll come up on the serial console.

> Which of the serial connectors should be the console (OPA0:)?

The one labeled 1.

> Apparently, this machine has two CPUs.  Is one of them comparable in 
> speed to the 255/233 (21064A)?

Yes, plus or minus being an EV4 instead of an EV45. (I don't know which
generation the 2100's CPUs are.) 


