In article <Xns949D9C1D84554noneherecom@127.0.0.1>, Nobodyishere <Nobodyishere@somebigisp.com> writes:
:"Jack Peacock" <peacock@simconv.com> wrote in

:> Sounds like a dead drive.  SCSI isn't like MFM where a reformat is the
:> fix technique of choice whenever problems show up.  It's been my
:> experience that low level format on a SCSI drive rarely if ever fixes
:> the problem.  A lot of drives the FORMAT command doesn't do anything.

  I concur; the drive sounds bad.

:Its not totally dead,  I can actually do some writes.  Seems to have bad 
:sectors.  But I guess it could be more serious.  Its actually a Quantum.  
:Do you think it might be worthwhile to put it in a MS-DOS machine and run
:the Maxtor/Quantum diagnostic software?

  Uncorrected bad sectors are seldom a good sign, and are seldom 
  recoverable through the ancient format incantations -- in my
  experience, it is best to swap the drive.  Ancient drives
  tended to have isolated failures and graceful degradation,
  SCSI and ATA drives tend to have recoverable errors or fatal
  problems, and you're reporting evidence of the latter case.

:Or what drives can I put in the VLC?  What's the largest?  

  Various RZ-series drives will work, probably up through the RZ28 or
  so.  (This due to thermal -- the SOC information that is available 
  via the OpenVMS FAQ will have the names of the drives the system 
  was sold with.)  If you have an external box, you can probably go
  larger than the RZ29, assuming sufficient power and cooling.

  I have no idea what NetBSD might support here, but OpenVMS would
  would work with most any RZ2x series drive (assuming thermal limits
  are met), assuming OpenVMS V6.0 and later.  OpenVMS VAX V7.3 is
  current.  OpenVMS Hobbyist (free) licensing information is available
  via the pointers in the OpenVMS FAQ.


