In article <c20p8a$5u8$1@news3.bu.edu>, Igor Sheyn <sheyn@cs.bu.edu> writes:
>I have a DEC 90M inside a DEC MultiStack System.
>I reset it to factory default; when it starts booting, I ^B^B to break out
>of it and type 'b fla:mneng3'.  It says "Image load complete" in a few seconds, but 
>after that the console locks up - I can't type anything, pressing reset on the front
>panel doesn't work, have to pull the plug.
>Any idea?

Yup. You are obviously not patient enough.
The DECserver90M came in 2 flavours (IIRC -MA and -PA with 1MB and 2MB flash)
and therefore there are two identical image loads (MNENG2 and MNENG3) which
differ only in compressed vs uncompressed (IIRC MNENG2 was the compressed).

If a DECserver with the smaller flash has a image load in it, it is the
compressed variant (regardless of the name), which means also it needs
quite some time (about 5min !) to decompress it after loading it and
before starting it. When it finished decompression and starts the image
there is another message on the console.

You may have a compressed image, though it is named MNENG3...
(I did this also with mine, because I had a mixture of 1MB and 2MB variants
and all factory defaulted to MNENG3 and also my reset-script specified MNENG3).

AFAIK the reset switch is only read during power on (for a factory default)
and maybe during software run (but not during firmware run eg. decompression).

